      </nav>

      <article>
        <section class="method-section" id="rule">
          <span class="label">§ 01 · The rule</span>
          <h2>One declaration—or none.</h2>
          <p>A category resolves to one named model or an explicit EMPTY finding. Shortlists transfer the burden of judgment back to the reader. The register exists to make the judgment.</p>
          <div class="method-cards">
            <div class="method-card"><strong>Declared</strong><span>One specific model is named and defended.</span></div>
            <div class="method-card"><strong>Empty</strong><span>No available object presently satisfies the Form.</span></div>
            <div class="method-card"><strong>In review</strong><span>Evidence is incomplete, conditional, or the category must be divided.</span></div>
            <div class="method-card"><strong>Never</strong><span>No “top five,” tie, preference matrix, or affiliate shortlist.</span></div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="method-section" id="admission">
          <span class="label">§ 02 · Admission test</span>
          <h2>Five gates. All must hold.</h2>
          <div class="method-cards">
            <div class="method-card"><strong>01 · Stable Form</strong><span>The category represents one coherent job, not incompatible sub-forms.</span></div>
            <div class="method-card"><strong>02 · Durable core</strong><span>The useful body survives ordinary wear and is not designed around early replacement.</span></div>
            <div class="method-card"><strong>03 · Reversible failure</strong><span>Expected failures can be repaired, rebuilt, maintained, or rationally renewed.</span></div>
            <div class="method-card"><strong>04 · Stable model</strong><span>The declaration names a durable specification, supported lineage, or long-lived model.</span></div>
            <div class="method-card"><strong>05 · Ends the search</strong><span>The object reaches the category’s practical ceiling without relying on luxury theater.</span></div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="method-section" id="evidence">
          <span class="label">§ 03 · Evidence</span>
          <h2>Mechanisms, not adjectives.</h2>
          <p>A declaration needs multiple independent signals: material and construction, field longevity, spare-parts access, published service paths, professional adoption, long production history, or a warranty that genuinely restores use. Marketing claims are not evidence.</p>
          <div class="method-cards">
            <div class="method-card"><strong>Good evidence</strong><span>Replaceable parts, repair manuals, known failure mechanisms, production continuity.</span></div>
            <div class="method-card"><strong>Disqualifier</strong><span>Sealed construction, proprietary lock-in, disposable coatings, irreversible degradation.</span></div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="method-section" id="empty">
          <span class="label">§ 04 · Via negativa</span>
          <h2>Emptiness is a finding.</h2>
          <p>EMPTY is not missing content. It records that the market has failed to embody the Form. The dossier therefore names both the failure and the conditions a future product would have to meet. The absence is described as precisely as a declaration.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="method-section" id="revocation">
          <span class="label">§ 05 · Revocation</span>
          <h2>No declaration is immortal.</h2>
          <p>A model leaves the register when its construction changes, parts disappear, serviceability collapses, evidence proves weaker than claimed, or a more complete embodiment reaches the same Form. Revocation is public editorial maintenance—not embarrassment.</p>
        </section>
      </article>
    </div>
